All three scored 14 of 14 on the spec probe, including the clause taken verbatim from the real
incident: an arm QUOTING `error: rate limit exceeded` in its summary, with exit 0 and 396 lines,
must be arm_result. And all three converged on the two decisions the spec left to them: an
unreaped run (`exit_code: None`) is Unknown, and an UNMEASURED line count falls through to
arm_result rather than excusing the arm, because the shape rule requires a measured zero.

THE FIRST REAL DISCOVERY FROM CROSS-EXAMINATION IN THIS SESSION, hours after the
strip_test_modules fix made the instrument run at all:

    glm-53-flash   discovery=1  broke=['claude-sonnet']
    codex-luna     survived 1 of 1
    claude-sonnet  survived 0 of 1

AND IT IS NOT A DEFECT. The failing test is `an_unstripped_head_is_normalised_again_before_
matching`: glm passes a log head still carrying `\x1b[91m` and asserts QuotaLimited. My spec
says, in the field's own doc comment, "The head of the run's own log, already ANSI-stripped by
the caller." claude-sonnet implements that contract exactly. glm implements a superset and tests
the superset, which my rubric names precisely -- "your tests may not assert on cases outside the
list, because another correct implementation may reasonably not handle them."

So the matrix recorded a discovery where the specification was not violated, and crossx's own
overfitted flag reads False for glm: its heuristic is breaking N-1 of N, and breaking 1 of 3 does
not trip it. A suite CAN discriminate by testing outside the spec, and with a small field the
existing check cannot tell that from a real finding. Worth knowing before the next time a
discovery decides a task.

MERGED glm ANYWAY, and on evidence rather than on the discovery. Its defensive re-normalisation
is exactly the belt-and-braces this session paid for twice: the colour incident happened because
text reached a matcher unstripped, and the OSC incident happened because a payload survived
stripping. A classifier that normalises its own input cannot be broken by a caller that forgets.
It also carries the most tests, 41 to 37 to 31, and is the only one of the three on a free route.

